"The postoperative pathology reveals a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) diagnosis with pathological stage pT2c. A reassuring finding is the negative surgical margins, as documented by the surgical pathology report stating, \"surgical margins were negative.\" Furthermore, the seminal vesicles were not invaded, and lymphovascular invasion was absent. Conversely, nodal status is uncertain as \"no lymph nodes were removed,\" meaning the pNx status does not establish node-negative disease. Biopsy findings showed Gleason 3+4 and 3+3 patterns. The prognosis is influenced by the negative margins and lack of seminal vesicle invasion. However, the absence of documented postoperative PSA means biochemical recurrence cannot be assessed. Missing nodal sampling widens uncertainty regarding regional disease control. Postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory are critical information that would revise this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 74.28 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 44.57–103.99 months; its coverage has not been validated."
